                <pb facs="0747" n="741" sameAs="frus:frus1894app1/pg741"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2 in No. 961.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Samoa</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1893-04-04"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Tuesday
                        Night</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#center">[The Standard, Wednesday, April 5, 1893.]</p>
                <p rendition="#center">[From our correspondent.]</p>
                <p>The chief judge of Samoa, the Swedish lawyer, Baron Cedercrantz, has repeated his
                    request to be released from his office on the ground that the Berlin-Samoan
                    treaty appears to be a farce, and that he can do no good with his present
                    situation. His wish in this matter will, I believe, shortly be complied
                    with.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
